<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ammonius Saccas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ammonius Saccas was a Greek philosopher who taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in the first half of the third century CE. He left no writings, but his pupils included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a>, who studied with him for about eleven years, and probably the Christian theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a>. He is often regarded as a forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ammonius Saccas was a self-taught philosopher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in Roman Egypt, generally regarded as a precursor or one of the founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a>. Most details of his life come from fragments of Porphyry's writings. The meaning of his cognomen is disputed. Many scholars take it to mean he was a porter in his youth, a view supported in antiquity by the Byzantine bishop Theodoret, while others have connected it with the Śākyas, an ancient ruling clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and argued for Indian origin.</p><p>Scholars who reject an Indian origin point out that Ammonius came from the Brucheion quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, the royal quarter inhabited mostly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and that the name Ammonius was common among Greeks. His name is also theophoric, honouring the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, which may indicate Egyptian origins. Porphyry says his parents were Christians and that he rejected their religion for paganism when he learned Greek philosophy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and Eusebius deny this and say he remained a Christian to the end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>According to Longinus, Ammonius wrote nothing, and little is known of his own philosophical views. Hierocles, writing in the 5th century, states that his fundamental doctrine was that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> were in full agreement. According to Nemesius, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a> around 400, he held that the soul is immaterial. Two of his pupils, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> the Pagan and Longinus, seem to have held positions closer to Middle Platonism than to Neoplatonism, which may suggest that Ammonius' own doctrines were closer to Middle Platonism too.</p><p>Some scholars explain the confusion about his faith by supposing there were two men called Ammonius, Ammonius Saccas who taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> and an Ammonius the Christian who wrote biblical texts. Ammonius is also said to have had two pupils named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a>, one Christian and one pagan, and Herennius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-longinus">Cassius Longinus</a> were among his other students.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His best-known pupil was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a>, who according to Porphyry went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 232 at the age of 28 to study philosophy. Disappointed by the teachers recommended to him, he was sent to Ammonius and said to a friend, This is the man I was looking for. He stayed with Ammonius for eleven years, and Porphyry says he then became eager to learn the Persian and Indian disciplines of philosophy.</p><p>Ammonius was undoubtedly the most significant influence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plotinus">Plotinus</a> in his development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a>, although his own role in its development is little known. Porphyry suggests Ammonius helped Plotinus think about philosophy in new ways, and Plotinus does not seem to have thought he departed in any significant way from his master's teaching.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ammonius Saccas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Claudius Ptolemy wrote the Almagest and the Geography there in the second century, Plotinus studied under the philosopher <b>Ammonius Saccas</b> before founding Neoplatonism in Rome, and Hero of Alexandria described machines and devices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=264" aria-label="Alexandria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5322 1027 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ammonius Saccas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.2,29.89&amp;year=264">What was here in 264 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
